Final Combined Report
CivicAge Survey App - ICSSR Data Collection Workflow Integration
Project: Smart Cities, Silent Citizens
Funding/Scheme: ICSSR Major Research Project 2025-26
Domain: Legal Studies - Elder Abuse and Protection
Field cities: New Delhi, Srinagar, Aizawl, Chennai, Gandhinagar, Indore
Target demographic: Oldest Old Population aged 75+ years, with the current instrument kit operationalising several tools around 75-85 years
App: CivicAge Survey (CAS)
1. Core Idea of the App
The CivicAge Survey app should be understood as a fieldwork operating system for ethical, accessible, and legally defensible research with the oldest old population. The study is not only collecting answers; it is documenting how digital-by-default smart city governance affects autonomy, dignity, well-being, service access, social participation, and dependence among older persons.
The app therefore has to do five jobs at the same time:
**Guide field investigators** through every consent, questionnaire, interview, FGD, observation, and QA step.
**Protect vulnerable participants** by making participation voluntary, skippable, stoppable, low-risk, and fatigue-sensitive.
**Convert the full instrument kit into a structured workflow**, including 17 primary instruments, 4 consent forms, 4 QA tools, and 2 annexures.
**Secure the research data** through participant codes, locked linkage files, anonymisation, role-based access, audio deletion, and QA approval before export.
**Produce reliable research evidence** that can support legal, policy, urban governance, and gerontological findings without exposing individual participants or institutions.
In simple terms: CAS is not just a survey app. It is the digital field protocol for the entire ICSSR project.
2. Why the App Is Needed
The research concerns older persons who may face physical, cognitive, sensory, literacy, language, and confidence barriers in digital environments. Ordinary survey tools are not enough because they do not enforce consent, privacy, breaks, local language adaptation, caregiver separation, observation ethics, audio deletion, or anonymised reporting. CAS must therefore embed the research protocol directly into the software.
The app should make it impossible or difficult to commit core protocol errors such as:
starting a tool without consent;
recording audio without audio consent;
administering SQ-OOP and SSI-OOP in the same session;
forgetting the 25-minute break;
storing names inside research answers;
exporting a record before QA;
retaining audio after transcription;
publishing institution names without permission;
treating skipped items as missing data.
